# Propose Conventional Commit after the task

When a task is done and it produced (or will produce) changes in the repository,
end the reply with a proposed commit message. Do **not** create the commit unless
the user explicitly asks.

## When to propose

- Code, docs, config, skills, rules, or other tracked files were added, edited, or deleted.
- Skip if there is nothing to commit (no meaningful diff, pure Q&A, or the user
  already provided the commit message / asked not to suggest one).

## Message format

Follow [Conventional Commits 1.0.0](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/):

```text
<type>[optional scope][!]: <description>
```

- Prefer `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`, `ci`, `build`, `perf`, `style`.
- One dominant intent; if the diff mixes intents, propose separate messages or say how to split.
- Description: short, imperative, why/intent — not a file list.
- Add a body or `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer only when needed.
- Prefer lowercase type; keep the subject concise (about 72 characters).

## How to present

At the end of the completion reply, add a short block, for example:

```text
feat(rules): propose Conventional Commit after each completed task
```

If the skill `conventional-commits` is available, follow it for drafting and validation.

Do not add `Co-authored-by` trailers (including Cursor / `cursoragent@cursor.com`).
